WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:05.960
The next library we will cover is the random
library, which provides several ways to generate

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:10.020
random numbers or a random value from a list
of values.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:14.960
Random numbers play an important role in gaming
as many games need, as part of their flow,

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:20.920
different events to happen each time one plays
those games to keep them fun and interesting.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:24.970
As we have many random library pieces to cover
in this topic, I won’t worry about making

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:30.000
these relate to gaming in detail but understand
that many games need a randomizer to ask a

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.730
random question or position a player in a
random spot.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:38.700
For any of these random library methods, the
random library needs to be imported, and,

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:46.360
in this 623-random file, that import takes
place on line 1 with the from random statement.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:53.030
Then, we see that the first method we cover,
randrange, is imported.

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:57.570
A for statement is set on line 2 to run 10
times.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:03.739
The randrange method returns a number between
the first number in the range and the last

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:09.340
number minus one, just like the second number
in a range for a for statement.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:16.119
I will run this code and we see 10 numbers
between 5 and 19.

00:01:16.119 --> 00:01:21.700
As is the case with for loops, we can add
a step to the random number range.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:27.090
For example, in a game, one team has all odd
numbers and the other has all even numbers.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:36.200
I will add a comma and 2 to the randrange
method, rerun the code, and we see all odd

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:43.840
numbers in the result, as the first number
in the range, 5, is odd.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:49.090
If I change the first number to a six and
rerun the code, the numbers are even because

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:54.190
we ask for random numbers in steps of 2.

00:01:54.190 --> 00:02:00.229
The next random library method, randint, returns
an integer between two specified numbers,

00:02:00.229 --> 00:02:05.770
and, unlike the randrange method, includes
the last number in the range.

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:17.110
I will change the randrange method to randint
and the content in the parentheses to 1 comma

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:19.620
20.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:30.260
I will rerun the code, and we see an error
message indicating that randint is not defined?

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:31.260
Why?

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:38.690
What I have done here can be a common occurrence
when we use a library and import a specific

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:40.300
module.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:45.940
On line 1, the randrange module is being imported.

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:56.310
We need this module to be randint, so I will
make the change, rerun the code, and the code

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.190
works perfectly.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:05.569
Next, let's look at the random method, which
returns a floating point decimal between 0

00:03:05.569 --> 00:03:07.360
and 1.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:13.130
I will change the library module being imported
to random, on line 1.

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:19.290
Then, I will set the print statement to print
the random method to run the method that generates

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:23.659
that floating point number.

00:03:23.659 --> 00:03:31.439
I will run this code and we see 10 random
numbers between 0 and 1.

00:03:31.439 --> 00:03:34.640
When would we use this type of random number?

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:38.970
We could use this random number to give a
bonus opportunity in a game a percentage of

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:42.319
the time, like 25% of the time.

00:03:42.319 --> 00:03:50.040
I will add some logic to this code to indicate
that if the random number is .75 or higher,

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.629
to print a message indicating it is bonus
time.

00:03:52.629 --> 00:03:57.159
First, I will change line three to store the
random number generated in a variable, since

00:03:57.159 --> 00:04:01.290
random by itself is not a variable.

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:09.329
Then, I will add an if statement to see if
the random number, x, is at least .75, meaning

00:04:09.329 --> 00:04:14.709
it’s in the highest 25 percent, and add
a print bonus statement.

00:04:14.709 --> 00:04:23.120
I will run this code several times, and we
seem to be averaging hitting the bonus twice

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:24.080
to three times per run.

